{
  "case_id": "2018-18-457",
  "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0000-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:07 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "North Carolina Department of Revenue",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SAWCHAK: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: This case involves a statute that taxes trust income in proportion to the interests of in-state beneficiaries. Now, trust beneficiaries, it bears remembering, are the true owners of trust income under trust law. Because of all the benefits and protections that states extend to their residents, the states' pro rata tax on trust income comports with due process."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: You couldn't -- you couldn't tax the beneficiaries on that accumulated income when they haven't received it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Look, the trustee lives in New York, okay? The settlor is in New York. All the administration is in New York. There is one thing that's going to happen in North Carolina. The thing that's going to happen in North Carolina is if she is there when it's distributed, she'll get some money. Okay? Which you're totally free to tax. But that isn't what you want to tax. You want to tax all these things which are everyone except her is in New York, and moreover, we don't even know if she'll ever get the money. Now there's something wrong with that. I don't know, it doesn't say specifically about trusts in the Constitution, but, thus, I mean, lots of trusts say there are 10 beneficiaries, each one lives in a different state, and I, the trustee, have total discretion as to who give this money to and maybe I'll give it to none of them. So here's a woman who might get none of it, and you want to tax that. Is that right? Do I have the facts right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Does it have to?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Would your position be different if she were -- if -- if the -- if the trustee did not have this discretion as to"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: So the state of administration can tax the income?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. O'Neil",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0002-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If there is no contingency, if the beneficiary has an absolute right to the income in a particular year, the state of residence absolutely can tax that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What -- what is the uncertainty, other than she has to stay alive?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "She might be in a different state when it goes to her. That's part of your point too, isn't it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Are there other instances in tax law where a resident of state B -- state A, is taxed by state A, on the basis of an increase of value of property that is located in state B?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. O'Neil",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0002-0010",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We believe throw-back taxes are permissible because they are taxing the beneficiary resident on money in their hands at that point."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Can North Carolina tax that increase in value?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. O'Neil",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0002-0011",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "A state could enact a wealth tax if the -- if the person that they are taxing has a current vested right to that and it's something that they could sell. It's alienable."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. O'Neil",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0002-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The trust instrument here is important to the facts of this case and to the outcome. Here, the trustee has absolute discretion, and what that means is that she is not guaranteed ever to receive --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. O'Neil",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0002-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If the beneficiary had a vested right in a certain percentage of the trust, then we would treat her as having a current interest in that trust."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Could -- could you just say a few words as to what the theoretical justification is for why New York and Connecticut can tax the growth of these assets?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 63,
        "start": 63
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Could both impose a tax? And I guess they have credit mechanisms."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: What do you do with Mr. O'Neil's canonical case of Hanson against Denckla?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: Well, we've never suggested, though, that tax jurisdiction exceeds adjudicative jurisdiction, have we? It's usually the other way around."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0003-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 69,
        "start": 69
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: Are you aware of a case where we've said that tax jurisdiction is broader than adjudicative jurisdiction?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Heritage Reporting Corporation",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0003-0005",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 69,
        "start": 69
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Fundamentally, this is a due process challenge. And I would -- I would drive home again that the point that my colleague is promoting in terms of a rule under the Due Process Clause would invalidate the trust tax criteria in 33 states. That would be an aggressive deployment of the Due Process Clause."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2018-18-457",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b95094278ea0a0ae23a34ee9a37691e5536b8fbd83f48470928be4f96efb3056",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b95094278ea0-0003-0006",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 70,
        "start": 70
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "(Whereupon, at 12:09 p.m. , the case was submitted.)"
    },
    {
